VERDICT
Partly correct: the logic works and passes on LeetCode by using a 64-bit long long, but it violates the problem's strict constraint that assumes the environment cannot store 64-bit integers.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You handled negative numbers naturally because in modern C++ (C++11 onward), the modulo operator % on negative numbers retains the negative sign (for example, -123 % 10 is -3).

You used standard digit extraction arithmetic (rev * 10 + digit and x / 10).

You correctly checked boundaries against INT_MAX and INT_MIN to return 0 when an overflow occurs.

Is it correct?

It passes all test cases on LeetCode's online judge because LeetCode runs on a 64-bit architecture where long long is 64 bits.

However, the problem explicitly states: "Assume the environment does not allow you to store 64-bit integers (signed or unsigned)." Under that restriction, declaring long long rev is not permitted.

Failing test scenario: Any system or test harness strictly restricted to 32-bit registers.

Line causing the constraint violation:

C++
long long rev = 0;
Fix to keep all variables strictly within standard 32-bit signed int:

C++
int rev = 0;
while (x != 0) {
    int digit = x % 10;
    if (rev > INT_MAX / 10 || (rev == INT_MAX / 10 && digit > 7)) return 0;
    if (rev < INT_MIN / 10 || (rev == INT_MIN / 10 && digit < -8)) return 0;
    rev = rev * 10 + digit;
    x /= 10;
}
return rev;
Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

Checking for overflow after the number has already grown past 32 bits requires a larger variable type. In interviews, this is considered evading the overflow challenge rather than solving it.

Edge cases handled properly: 0 (returns 0), numbers ending in 0 like 120 (returns 21), single-digit numbers, negative numbers like -123 (returns -321), and large numbers like 1534236469 (which overflows 32-bit int and returns 0).

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(log10(|x|)) because each iteration divides x by 10, running at most 10 times for any 32-bit signed integer.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because only two scalar variables (rev and digit) are stored.

Why my approach works, or why it doesn't:

It works mathematically because multiplying the previous result by 10 shifts existing digits to the left, making room to add the next popped digit to the units place. Using a 64-bit container accommodates numbers larger than 2,147,483,647 without crashing before your bounds check runs.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: String Conversion with Exception Handling (Brute Force)

Where it comes from:

Beginner baseline often seen in LeetCode discussion forums.

Idea:

Convert the integer to a string. Reverse the string (ignoring the negative sign if present). Convert it back to an integer using std::stoi inside a try-catch block, returning 0 if std::out_of_range is thrown.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <stdexcept>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int reverse(int x) {
        string s = to_string(x);
        bool isNegative = false;
        if (s[0] == '-') {
            isNegative = true;
            s = s.substr(1);
        }
        std::reverse(s.begin(), s.end());
        try {
            int result = stoi(s);
            return isNegative ? -result : result;
        } catch (const out_of_range&) {
            return 0;
        }
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(|x|)) to convert to string, reverse characters, and parse back.

Space Complexity:

O(log10(|x|)) auxiliary heap/stack memory to store the string buffer.

What makes the next approach better:

Converting to text involves dynamic string memory allocation and exception-handling overhead. We can reverse the number mathematically.

Approach 2: Full Reversal Using 64-bit Integer (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

Classic numerical reversal found on GeeksforGeeks and introductory programming sites.

Idea:

Extract digits with % 10 and assemble them into a 64-bit long long integer. Once all digits are assembled, check if the value falls outside [INT_MIN, INT_MAX].

C++ Code:

C++
#include <climits>

class Solution {
public:
    int reverse(int x) {
        long long rev = 0;
        while (x != 0) {
            rev = rev * 10 + (x % 10);
            x /= 10;
        }
        if (rev > INT_MAX || rev < INT_MIN) {
            return 0;
        }
        return (int)rev;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(|x|)) since it takes at most 10 iterations for a 32-bit integer.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space using primitive scalar variables.

What makes the next approach better:

It relies on a 64-bit type (long long). We can enforce overflow checking before doing the multiplication so that no variable ever exceeds 32 bits.

Approach 3: Pre-Multiplication Overflow Checks with 32-bit Integers (Optimal)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial and canonical interview solution.

Idea:

We want to do rev = rev * 10 + digit without causing integer overflow.

32-bit signed integers range from -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647.

Before doing rev * 10, check if rev > INT_MAX / 10 (or rev < INT_MIN / 10). If so, multiplying by 10 will definitely overflow.

If rev == INT_MAX / 10, adding a digit greater than 7 will overflow.

If rev == INT_MIN / 10, adding a digit less than -8 will overflow.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <climits>

class Solution {
public:
    int reverse(int x) {
        int rev = 0;
        while (x != 0) {
            int digit = x % 10;
            if (rev > INT_MAX / 10 || (rev == INT_MAX / 10 && digit > 7)) {
                return 0;
            }
            if (rev < INT_MIN / 10 || (rev == INT_MIN / 10 && digit < -8)) {
                return 0;
            }
            rev = rev * 10 + digit;
            x /= 10;
        }
        return rev;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(|x|)) running at most 10 iterations.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space strictly using standard 32-bit signed integers.

What makes the next approach better:

A 32-bit integer has at most 10 digits. The only 10-digit numbers whose reverse could overflow start with a digit of 2 or higher. Since a valid 32-bit signed input cannot start with a digit greater than 2, the boundary checks digit > 7 and digit < -8 can never be triggered in practice on valid inputs, allowing a slightly streamlined check.

Approach 4: Streamlined Pre-Check (Interview Micro-Optimization)

Where it comes from:

Top-voted LeetCode community discussions analyzing 32-bit input properties.

Idea:

INT_MAX is 2,147,483,647 and INT_MIN is -2,147,483,648. Any 10-digit input x that does not overflow when entered must start with 1 or 2. When reversed, its last digit added will be 1 or 2, which is well below 7 and above -8. Thus, if rev == INT_MAX / 10, digit can only ever be 1 or 2 (or -1 or -2). Checking simply rev > INT_MAX / 10 and rev < INT_MIN / 10 is mathematically sufficient for all valid inputs.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <climits>

class Solution {
public:
    int reverse(int x) {
        int rev = 0;
        while (x != 0) {
            if (rev > INT_MAX / 10 || rev < INT_MIN / 10) {
                return 0;
            }
            rev = rev * 10 + (x % 10);
            x /= 10;
        }
        return rev;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(|x|)) loop iterations.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space using purely 32-bit signed integers.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 2 (Full Reversal Using 64-bit Integer).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers specifically ask this problem to test your ability to handle integer overflow without 64-bit types. They expect Approach 3 (Pre-Multiplication Overflow Checks). Explaining the insight in Approach 4 shows a deeper understanding of input constraints.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: Why 7 and -8 in the checks rev == INT_MAX / 10 && digit > 7?

Answer: INT_MAX is 2,147,483,647 (ends in 7) and INT_MIN is -2,147,483,648 (ends in -8). If rev equals 214,748,364, rev * 10 is 2,147,483,640. Adding anything greater than 7 overflows the positive bound, and adding anything less than -8 overflows the negative bound.

Question 2: Can rev == INT_MAX / 10 ever occur with digit > 7 from a valid 32-bit input?

Answer: No. For a 10-digit number to end with a digit greater than 7 after reversal, the original number must have started with a digit greater than 7. But the maximum 32-bit signed integer starts with 2 (2,147,483,647). Therefore, any valid 10-digit input starts with 1 or 2, so the last extracted digit will only ever be 1 or 2.

Question 3: How does C++ handle modulo on negative numbers?

Answer: Since C++11, integer division truncates toward zero, which guarantees that x == (x / 10) * 10 + (x % 10). Consequently, -123 % 10 evaluates to -3, allowing positive and negative inputs to be handled with identical arithmetic.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

To prevent 32-bit integer overflow before executing rev * 10 + digit, verify that rev does not exceed INT_MAX / 10 or drop below INT_MIN / 10.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Check problem constraints for prohibitions on 64-bit integer types before reaching for long long.

Place overflow checks before the multiplying operation, not after.

Remember that INT_MAX % 10 is 7 and INT_MIN % 10 is -8.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Digit manipulation and boundary checking to prevent arithmetic overflow.

THE BEST SOLUTION

